// Phase 6 — row-polymorphic effects.
//
// A function may declare an EFFECT ROW after its return type: `| {IO, Net}`.
// The row names the side effects the function is permitted to perform. The
// compiler then checks SUBSUMPTION (`axon check`): at every call site inside a
// row-declaring function, the effects the callee performs — computed
// transitively, so nothing can hide behind a helper — must be a subset of the
// caller's declared row. A leak is a static error (E1310).
//
// Effects are ERASED before codegen, so `axon run` == `axon build` and there is
// zero runtime cost. During the migration window a function with NO row clause
// is unconstrained; you opt into checking by writing a row.
//
// Run:   axon run   examples/effects.ax
// Build: axon build examples/effects.ax

// A pure computation. The explicit empty row `| {}` opts in and promises NO
// effects — the compiler holds it to that.
fn double(n: i64) -> i64 | {} {
    n + n
}

// A function that prints MUST declare `IO`, or `axon check` rejects it. The
// declared row documents exactly what the function may do.
fn announce(label: str, n: i64) -> i64 | {IO} {
    println("{label} = {to_str(n)}")
    n
}

// Effects compose along the call graph. `report` itself only prints, but it
// calls `announce` (which is {IO}), so `report` must also declare `IO`. If it
// declared `| {}` here, the compiler would flag the transitive IO leak.
fn report(n: i64) -> i64 | {IO} {
    announce("doubled", double(n))
}

// `main` is the top-level escape hatch: it may perform any effect without an
// explicit row, so a whole program stays ergonomic while its subordinate
// functions remain honestly annotated.
fn main() {
    let r = report(21)
    println("result = {to_str(r)}")
}
